anging workshop  actually  last year we did nine separate workshops  during one particular week instead of  that week's lecture  so we did actually me and my staff we  did as much work  that entire week that week as the entire  rest of the quarter combined  so i'd love to say i'm never doing that  again but i'm going to do it one more  time this year and i'll you'll see in a  few minutes why  students actually love it okay and i'm  not and you might think ah come on  there's a whole bunch of guys who aren't  going to get into this  not not so okay everybody gets into it  and  actually quite interesting  this particular person right here  you might not be able to tell but this  person is blind  how do you deal with a student with that  kind of disability in a course like this  well guess what it's about flowers  and these flowers to a blind person are  just as potent as to a seeing person  it's just they  sense something different okay  so her bouquet was going to be  she wasn't going to put stuff in and it  doesn't have scent all right she's gonna  anyway so  i learned a bunch of things from this  student i was really happy this student  came on board because i had no clue how  i would teach this material to a blind  person so it was actually  i may have learned more from  watching her  learn the material than than  than i would have thought  then in one lecture i give every student  a flowering pot of plant this happens  about the third week of the quarter  and the student is giving given this  plant  and with the instruction that they have  to make some impact with it  and the first question that  usually comes back is can i make impact  on myself and  what i usually say to this well you're  going to have a writing assignment where  you have to describe what impact you  made with it  and if you want to write about how it  made an impact on you  that's fine but i would caution you that  that might be a little bit more  difficult than giving it to your mother  and y